AHS-006 — Component Model

Document ID: AHS-006
Title: Component Model
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

Architectural Views: Logical

1. Purpose

This specification defines the canonical component model of the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The component model establishes the structural building blocks of Atlas, their responsibilities, interfaces, lifecycle, and interaction rules. It enables modular implementation, independent evolution, and interoperability while preserving the canonical architecture defined by ACC and AHS.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Component types
Component responsibilities
Component interfaces
Component lifecycle
Component dependencies
Component interaction rules

It does not define implementation technologies or deployment strategies.

3. Canonical Definition

An Atlas Component is a modular architectural unit that encapsulates a well-defined engineering responsibility, exposes standardized interfaces, and collaborates with other components to implement engineering cognition.

A component is the smallest independently replaceable architectural unit.

4. Component Principles

All Atlas components SHALL:

Have a single, clearly defined responsibility.
Expose only documented interfaces.
Hide internal implementation details.
Be independently testable.
Support traceability and explainability where applicable.
Preserve the semantics defined by ACC.
5. Component Categories

Atlas defines four canonical component categories.

5.1 Cognitive Components

Implement engineering cognition.

Examples:

Observation Engine
Evidence Engine
Understanding Engine
Memory Engine
Knowledge Engine
Reasoning Engine
Capability Engine
Decision Support Engine
5.2 Governance Components

Provide cross-cutting governance.

Examples:

Context Manager
Intent Manager
Constraint Manager
Confidence Manager
Traceability Manager
Explainability Manager
5.3 Platform Components

Provide shared platform services.

Examples:

Workflow Orchestrator
Event Bus
Identity & Access Manager
Configuration Manager
Audit Manager
Telemetry Manager
Knowledge Registry
5.4 Integration Components

Connect Atlas with external systems.

Examples:

CAD Connectors
BIM Connectors
PLM Connectors
ERP Connectors
Sensor Gateways
API Gateway
File Import/Export Services
6. Component Architecture
                 External Systems
                        │
                Integration Components
                        │
                        ▼
            Platform Components
                        │
                        ▼
           Governance Components
                        │
                        ▼
             Cognitive Components

Each category provides services to the category below it while maintaining well-defined interfaces.

7. Component Lifecycle

Every component progresses through the following lifecycle.

Created
    │
Configured
    │
Initialized
    │
Ready
    │
Executing
    │
Paused
    │
Resumed
    │
Stopping
    │
Stopped
    │
Retired

A component SHALL expose its current lifecycle state.

8. Component Interface Model

Every component SHALL expose one or more standardized interfaces.

Input Interface

Receives requests, commands, or engineering artifacts.

Output Interface

Produces engineering artifacts or responses.

Configuration Interface

Manages configuration and policies.

Monitoring Interface

Reports health, status, metrics, and diagnostics.

Administration Interface

Supports lifecycle management and maintenance.

9. Component Interaction Rules

Components SHALL communicate only through published interfaces.

Components SHALL NOT:

Directly access another component's internal state.
Modify another component's data without an exposed interface.
Assume implementation details of another component.

Communication SHALL preserve:

Traceability
Explainability
Security
Context
10. Dependency Rules

Permitted dependencies:

Integration
      │
Platform
      │
Governance
      │
Cognitive

Components MAY depend on components in the same or lower architectural layer only if explicitly defined by AHS.

Circular dependencies SHALL NOT exist.

11. Component Quality Attributes

Every component SHALL be evaluated against the following attributes.

Attribute	Description
Cohesion	Focus on a single responsibility
Coupling	Minimize dependencies
Reusability	Usable across multiple workflows
Replaceability	Can be substituted without architectural changes
Observability	Health and metrics are measurable
Reliability	Predictable behavior under expected conditions
Scalability	Supports increased workload
Maintainability	Easy to evolve and support
12. Conformance

A component conforms to this specification if it:

Implements a single architectural responsibility.
Exposes standardized interfaces.
Preserves ACC semantics.
Complies with AHS layering rules.
Supports lifecycle management.
Documents dependencies and interfaces.
13. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

Additional component categories
Specialized cognitive components
Distributed component models
Multi-agent component types

provided they remain consistent with the canonical component model.